{"componentChunkName":"component---src-templates-blog-post-tsx","path":"/blog/2024-05-10--advanced-automation-using-ansible-and-dorothy/","result":{"data":{"site":{"siteMetadata":{"disqus":"edward-j-schwartz"}},"post":{"htmlAst":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In my "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://edmcman.github.io/blog/2024-04-20--creating-a-modern-ish-usable-linux-desktop-with-notion/"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"last\npost"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nI talked about how I have been using Ansible for my new laptop configuration,\nand shared my configuration for notion."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"So far, I've been extremely happy with using Ansible for configuring my machine.\nPrior to using Ansible, I'd spend a fair amount of time creating detailed notes\nthat described what I did.  I estimate that creating Ansible recipes takes about\nthe same time as keeping good notes, and maybe even less.  That's because there\nare many existing "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://galaxy.ansible.com/ui/standalone/roles/"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"roles"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for\ncommon settings and software that can be reused.  As with any ecosystem, the\nquality of such roles varies."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The big difference between my notes and Ansible, though, is that Ansible\nplaybooks can be played in minutes, whilst manually following my notes can take\nhours to set up an entire new machine.  I used to "},{"type":"element","tagName":"em","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"dread"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the idea of\nconfiguring a new machine.  But now it's fairly effortless."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"I just "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/edmcman/ansible-files"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"publicly shared"}]},{"type":"text","value":" my Ansible\nconfiguration.  I don't expect that anyone will use my configuration as is, any\nmore than I expect anyone to use my notion configuration!  I'm extremely\nopinionated and picky.  But I do hope that it might give people some ideas, like\nhow to install\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/edmcman/ansible-files/blob/main/roles/base/tasks/packages/steps/llvm.yml"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"llvm"}]},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/edmcman/ansible-files/blob/main/roles/desktop/nvidia-cuda/tasks/main.yml"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"nvidia\ndrivers"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nand so on.  I know I personally found "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/GonzalezAndrew/ansible-desktop-configuration/"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"other people's\nrepositories"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nto be helpful."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In a very similar vein, I've started using\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/bevry/dorothy"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"dorothy"}]},{"type":"text","value":", which claims to allow you to \"...\nbring your dotfile commands and configuration to any shell.\"  Since I usually\nbut not always use "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://fishshell.com/"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"fish"}]},{"type":"text","value":", I've always been hesistant to\nwrite my own commands in fish.  Plus, I have been writing bash scripts for long\nenough that I'm decent at it, so it tends to be one of my go-tos.  Dorothy makes\nit easy to define variables and commands in such a way that they magically\nappear in all shells.  (Again, this is very useful for fish, which is not a\nposix-compliant shell.)  There's also a fair number of useful built-in commands.\nDorothy encourages users to split their dotfiles into public and private\nportions, and you can view my "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/edmcman/dotfiles"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"public dotfile\nhere"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Specifically, here are my\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/edmcman/dotfiles/tree/main/commands"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"custom commands"}]},{"type":"text","value":".  Some\nof these might be useful, such as "},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"setup-util-ghidra"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"setup-util-ghidrathon"}]},{"type":"text","value":". I've found that having a designated spot for these\ntypes of utility commands encourages me to write them, which ultimately saves me\ntime. "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://xkcd.com/1205/"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Usually."}]}]}],"data":{"quirksMode":false}},"excerpt":"In my last\npost\nI talked about how I have been using Ansible for my new laptop configuration,\nand shared my configuration for notion. So far…","timeToRead":2,"fields":{"slug":"/blog/2024-05-10--advanced-automation-using-ansible-and-dorothy/"},"frontmatter":{"tags":["ansible","linux"],"author":{"jsonId":"Edward J. Schwartz","bio":"Computer Security Researcher","twitter":"@edmcman","avatar":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"fixed","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/4ed6d/ed.png","srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/4ed6d/ed.png 80w","sizes":"80px"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/1deab/ed.webp 80w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"80px"}]},"width":80,"height":80}}}},"title":"Advanced Automation using Ansible and Dorothy","updatedDate":"May 10, 2024","image":null}},"recents":{"edges":[{"node":{"id":"5598ca3b-d22d-56d0-9b2c-927b22406d3f","fields":{"slug":"/blog/2024-11-14--notes-profiling/"},"timeToRead":1,"frontmatter":{"title":"Notes: Profiling","image":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"fixed","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/ebd79983ac72bcaee00df0c4edd8ef0e/912d0/image.png","srcSet":"/static/ebd79983ac72bcaee00df0c4edd8ef0e/912d0/image.png 300w,\n/static/ebd79983ac72bcaee00df0c4edd8ef0e/e916b/image.png 600w","sizes":"300px"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/ebd79983ac72bcaee00df0c4edd8ef0e/e35c0/image.webp 300w,\n/static/ebd79983ac72bcaee00df0c4edd8ef0e/0483b/image.webp 600w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"300px"}]},"width":300,"height":100}}},"author":{"jsonId":"Edward J. Schwartz","avatar":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"fixed","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/8ef0e/ed.png","srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/8ef0e/ed.png 36w,\n/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/b0e74/ed.png 72w","sizes":"36px"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/9a807/ed.webp 36w,\n/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/de323/ed.webp 72w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"36px"}]},"width":36,"height":36}}}}}}},{"node":{"id":"5fa5977c-5be8-5c71-90de-f4b445cfefc7","fields":{"slug":"/blog/2024-07-19--a-dog-on-the-run/"},"timeToRead":5,"frontmatter":{"title":"Dog on the Run","image":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageDa